IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `eia-core`

-- Employment Insurance Act (S.C. 1996, c. 23), Part I, "Interpretation": s 6. Layer 1 of piece ei1 (Sonnet 5.5).
-- Text: the unofficial plain-text rendering of the Justice Laws consolidation current to 2026-09-30 (lines of the Act's raw file).
-- Nothing here takes anybody's point of view; the seat layer is ei1-seat.l4.
--
-- CONVENTIONS (the keel's, from eia-core's NOTES): money is a NUMBER of dollars, a rate is a fraction written with %,
-- hours, weeks and days are NUMBERs of that unit, a judgment the Act hands to someone else is a BOOLEAN input named
-- for that judgment, and a fact outside this piece's range (Part I, ss 6 to 23.3(5)) is a plain input with a comment
-- "out of range: ... (piece X)". Every rule has its own GIVEN because the stable binary rejects a GIVEN on a section.

§ `Section 6(1) - definitions for Part I`

-- src:138 | disentitled means not entitled under section 13, 18, 21, 31, 32, 33, 36, 37, 49 or 50 or under the regulations; (inadmissible)
-- A claimant is "disentitled" when not entitled under any of the listed sections or under the regulations.
-- Sections 13, 18 and 21 are in this piece; sections 31 to 33, 36, 37, 49 and 50 are in piece ei2.
@ref Employment Insurance Act s 6(1) "disentitled" (src:138)
GIVEN `not entitled under section 13` IS A BOOLEAN
      `not entitled under section 18` IS A BOOLEAN
      `not entitled under section 21` IS A BOOLEAN
      -- out of range: sections 31, 32, 33, 36, 37, 49 and 50 (piece ei2)
      `not entitled under section 31` IS A BOOLEAN
      `not entitled under section 32` IS A BOOLEAN
      `not entitled under section 33` IS A BOOLEAN
      `not entitled under section 36` IS A BOOLEAN
      `not entitled under section 37` IS A BOOLEAN
      `not entitled under section 49` IS A BOOLEAN
      `not entitled under section 50` IS A BOOLEAN
      -- out of range: the Regulations (pieces er1 to er6), which make other persons not entitled
      `not entitled under the regulations` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 6(1) disentitled` IF
       `not entitled under section 13`
    OR `not entitled under section 18`
    OR `not entitled under section 21`
    OR `not entitled under section 31`
    OR `not entitled under section 32`
    OR `not entitled under section 33`
    OR `not entitled under section 36`
    OR `not entitled under section 37`
    OR `not entitled under section 49`
    OR `not entitled under section 50`
    OR `not entitled under the regulations`

-- src:139 | disqualified means disqualified under section 27 or 30; (exclu du bénéfice des prestations)
-- Sections 27 and 30 are in piece ei2.
@ref Employment Insurance Act s 6(1) "disqualified" (src:139)
GIVEN -- out of range: section 27 (piece ei2)
      `disqualified under section 27` IS A BOOLEAN
      -- out of range: section 30 (piece ei2)
      `disqualified under section 30` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 6(1) disqualified` IF
       `disqualified under section 27`
    OR `disqualified under section 30`

-- src:140 | initial claim for benefits means a claim made for the purpose of establishing a claimant’s benefit period; (demande initiale de prestations)
@ref Employment Insurance Act s 6(1) "initial claim for benefits" (src:140)
GIVEN `a claim made for the purpose of establishing a claimant's benefit period` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 6(1) initial claim for benefits` IF
    `a claim made for the purpose of establishing a claimant's benefit period`

-- src:141 | major attachment claimant means a claimant who qualifies to receive benefits and has 600 or more hours of insurable employment in their qualifying period; (prestataire de la première catégorie)
-- src:142 | minor attachment claimant means a claimant who qualifies to receive benefits and has fewer than 600 hours of insurable employment in their qualifying period; (prestataire de la deuxième catégorie)
-- The dividing line between the two attachment classes. The text states it (600 hours). Schedule II (piece ei6)
-- replaces the definitions for one case; that replacement is not made here.
@ref Employment Insurance Act s 6(1) "major attachment claimant", "minor attachment claimant" (src:141-142)
`s 6(1) the number of hours of insurable employment that separates a major attachment claimant from a minor one` MEANS 600

@ref Employment Insurance Act s 6(1) "major attachment claimant" (src:141)
GIVEN `the claimant qualifies to receive benefits` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the hours of insurable employment in the claimant's qualifying period` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 6(1) major attachment claimant` IF
        `the claimant qualifies to receive benefits`
    AND `the hours of insurable employment in the claimant's qualifying period` AT LEAST `s 6(1) the number of hours of insurable employment that separates a major attachment claimant from a minor one`

@ref Employment Insurance Act s 6(1) "minor attachment claimant" (src:142)
GIVEN `the claimant qualifies to receive benefits` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the hours of insurable employment in the claimant's qualifying period` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 6(1) minor attachment claimant` IF
        `the claimant qualifies to receive benefits`
    AND `the hours of insurable employment in the claimant's qualifying period` BELOW `s 6(1) the number of hours of insurable employment that separates a major attachment claimant from a minor one`

-- "qualifying period" (src:143) means the period described in section 8: see `s 8 the qualifying period`.
-- "waiting period" (src:144) means the one week described in section 13: see `s 13 a week of unemployment served as the waiting period`.

§ `Section 6(2) - rounding`

-- src:146 | (2) A reference in this Part to an amount equal to a percentage or fraction of earnings or benefits in a period shall be rounded to the nearest multiple of one dollar or, if the amount is equidistant from two multiples of one dollar, to the higher multiple.
-- Encoded once, in the shared core, as `s 6(2) and s 152.01(5) amount rounded to the nearest dollar`. Every rule of this
-- piece that works out a percentage or a fraction of earnings or benefits calls it and cites s 6(2); none restates it.

§ `Section 6(3) - hours of insurable employment`

-- src:148 | (3) For the purposes of this Part, the number of hours of insurable employment that a claimant has in any period shall be established as provided under section 55, subject to any regulations made under paragraph 54(z.1) allocating the hours to the claimant’s qualifying period.
-- The hours are "established as provided under section 55" (piece ei2), "subject to any regulations made under
-- paragraph 54(z.1)" that allocate them to the qualifying period (Regulations, pieces er2 and er3).
-- This piece does not work the hours out: it takes the figure so established as an input and names it here, so that
-- every rule that reads "hours of insurable employment in the qualifying period" reads one fact.
@ref Employment Insurance Act s 6(3) (src:148)
GIVEN -- out of range: section 55 (piece ei2) and the regulations made under paragraph 54(z.1)
      `the hours of insurable employment established under section 55 and allocated to the qualifying period by regulations made under paragraph 54(z.1)` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `s 6(3) the hours of insurable employment that a claimant has in the qualifying period` IS
    `the hours of insurable employment established under section 55 and allocated to the qualifying period by regulations made under paragraph 54(z.1)`

§ `Section 6(4) and 6(5) - employment that is not suitable`

-- src:150 | (4) For the purposes of paragraphs 18(1)(a) and 27(1)(a) to (c) and subsection 50(8), employment is not suitable employment for a claimant if
-- src:151 | (a) it arises in consequence of a work stoppage attributable to a labour dispute;
@ref Employment Insurance Act s 6(4)(a) (src:150-151)
GIVEN `it arises in consequence of a work stoppage attributable to a labour dispute` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 6(4)(a) employment arises in consequence of a work stoppage attributable to a labour dispute` IF
    `it arises in consequence of a work stoppage attributable to a labour dispute`

-- src:152 | (b) it is in the claimant’s usual occupation and is either at a lower rate of earnings or on conditions less favourable than those observed by agreement between employers and employees or, in the absence of any such agreement, than those recognized by good employers; or
-- "Lower rate of earnings or conditions less favourable than" a benchmark: the benchmark is the rate and conditions
-- "observed by agreement between employers and employees" or, "in the absence of any such agreement", those
-- "recognized by good employers". The four comparisons are facts about the job; this rule picks the benchmark.
-- It is shared by paragraph (4)(b) and by subsection (5), which uses the same benchmark.
@ref Employment Insurance Act s 6(4)(b) (src:152)
GIVEN `there is an agreement between employers and employees` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the rate of earnings is lower than the rate observed by that agreement` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the conditions are less favourable than those observed by that agreement` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the rate of earnings is lower than the rate recognized by good employers` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the conditions are less favourable than those recognized by good employers` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 6(4)(b) and s 6(5) at a lower rate of earnings or on conditions less favourable than the benchmark` IF
    IF `there is an agreement between employers and employees`
    THEN `the rate of earnings is lower than the rate observed by that agreement`
         OR `the conditions are less favourable than those observed by that agreement`
    ELSE `the rate of earnings is lower than the rate recognized by good employers`
         OR `the conditions are less favourable than those recognized by good employers`

@ref Employment Insurance Act s 6(4)(b) (src:152)
GIVEN `it is in the claimant's usual occupation` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is at a lower rate of earnings or on conditions less favourable than the benchmark` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 6(4)(b) employment in the claimant's usual occupation is at a lower rate or on less favourable conditions` IF
        `it is in the claimant's usual occupation`
    AND `it is at a lower rate of earnings or on conditions less favourable than the benchmark`

-- src:153 | (c) it is not in the claimant’s usual occupation and is either at a lower rate of earnings or on conditions less favourable than those that the claimant might reasonably expect to obtain, having regard to the conditions that the claimant usually obtained in their usual occupation, or would have obtained if they had continued to be so employed.
@ref Employment Insurance Act s 6(4)(c) (src:153)
GIVEN `it is not in the claimant's usual occupation` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is at a lower rate of earnings than the claimant might reasonably expect` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is on conditions less favourable than the claimant might reasonably expect` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 6(4)(c) employment outside the claimant's usual occupation is at a lower rate or on less favourable conditions than the claimant might reasonably expect` IF
        `it is not in the claimant's usual occupation`
    AND (   `it is at a lower rate of earnings than the claimant might reasonably expect`
         OR `it is on conditions less favourable than the claimant might reasonably expect`)

-- src:155 | (5) After a lapse of a reasonable interval from the date on which an insured person becomes unemployed, paragraph (4)(c) does not apply to the employment described in that paragraph if it is employment at a rate of earnings not lower and on conditions not less favourable than those observed by agreement between employers and employees or, in the absence of any such agreement, than those recognized by good employers.
-- "A reasonable interval" is not measured in the Act. Whether one has lapsed is a BOOLEAN input (a judgment of the
-- Commission); the rule never works it out.
@ref Employment Insurance Act s 6(5) (src:155)
GIVEN `a reasonable interval has lapsed from the date on which the insured person became unemployed` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the employment is at a rate of earnings not lower and on conditions not less favourable than the benchmark` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 6(5) paragraph 6(4)(c) does not apply after a reasonable interval` IF
        `a reasonable interval has lapsed from the date on which the insured person became unemployed`
    AND `the employment is at a rate of earnings not lower and on conditions not less favourable than the benchmark`

-- src:150 | (4) For the purposes of paragraphs 18(1)(a) and 27(1)(a) to (c) and subsection 50(8), employment is not suitable employment for a claimant if
@ref Employment Insurance Act s 6(4) (src:150)
GIVEN `paragraph (a) applies` IS A BOOLEAN
      `paragraph (b) applies` IS A BOOLEAN
      `paragraph (c) applies` IS A BOOLEAN
      `subsection (5) takes paragraph (c) out of play` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 6(4) employment is not suitable employment` IF
        `paragraph (a) applies`
    OR `paragraph (b) applies`
    OR (`paragraph (c) applies` AND NOT (`subsection (5) takes paragraph (c) out of play`))
